| `assert` on the index | [[3.07 Input Validation and assert]] |

Two details deserve a closer look.

**One assignment operator serves both copy and move.** It takes its parameter **by value**. When the argument is an lvalue, the parameter is built by the copy constructor. When it is an rvalue, it is built by the move constructor. Either way, the function swaps contents with it and lets it carry the old data away. That is copy and swap again.

**`begin()` and `end()` make the range-based loop work.** A range-based `for` needs nothing more than those two functions returning something that behaves like an iterator, and a plain pointer does.

## `std::initializer_list`

```
 IntArray a{ 1, 2, 3 };
            └───┬───┘
    the compiler builds a hidden constant array [1, 2, 3]
    and passes a small view of it: std::initializer_list<int>
        with  begin()   end()   size()
```

A constructor taking a `std::initializer_list<T>` is what lets a class be initialized from a braced list of any length.

> [!warning] Braces prefer the list constructor
> If a class has an `initializer_list` constructor, brace initialization chooses it whenever it can. That is the reason for the rule in [[5.01 std::vector Basics]]:
> `std::vector<int> v{ 5 };` is one element with the value 5.
> `std::vector<int> v(5);` is five elements.
> `IntArray` has the same behaviour, which is why `resize` writes `IntArray replacement(size);` with parentheses.

## What a real vector does better

`push_back` here reallocates and copies **every time**, so adding `n` elements costs O(n²) in total. `std::vector` keeps a capacity larger than its size and grows it by a constant factor when full, making `push_back` amortized constant time ([[5.03 Vector as a Stack, and Capacity]]). Adding that is the natural next step.

## Check yourself
```quiz
? Why can one `operator=(IntArray other)` handle both copy and move assignment?
- Because the compiler converts all moves into copies
+ The parameter is copied or moved, depending on the argument
- Because `IntArray` has no move constructor
- Because assignment never needs to copy
> The caller's argument decides how `other` is built. The body is the same swap either way.

? What does a class need for a range-based `for` loop to work on it?
- An `operator[]` and a `size()` function
- To inherit from `std::vector`
+ `begin()` and `end()` functions that return iterators
- A constructor taking an `std::initializer_list`
> The range-based `for` calls `begin()` and `end()`. For an array-backed class, pointers to the first and one-past-last elements will do.

? Why does `IntArray replacement(size);` use parentheses?
- Because braces cannot be used with a variable
- Because braces would make the array `const`
- Because parentheses are faster
+ Braces would pick the `initializer_list` constructor
> Braces prefer an `initializer_list` constructor when there is one, so `{ size }` means "the list containing `size`".

? Why is this `push_back` slow compared with `std::vector`'s?
- Because it checks every element first
- Because it allocates on the stack
+ It reallocates and copies everything on every call
- Because it uses `delete[]` instead of `delete`
> Growing by one each time makes n insertions cost O(n²). Doubling the capacity makes the average `push_back` constant time.
```
